  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** is an adjective meaning **slow or lazy**. More specifically, it means **averse to activity, effort, or movement; habitually lazy**.\n\nAn indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running your corporation or competing with you in a relay race.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Medical Usage\n\nThe word also has a specific use in medicine. Doctors use the word **indolent** to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\nIn pathology, indolent can mean **causing little pain**, or (especially of a painless ulcer) **slow to heal**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Etymology (Word Origin)\n\nThe word comes from **Latin** *indol\u0113ns*, meaning \"not feeling pain,\" from *in-* (not) + *dol\u0113ns*, from *dol\u0113re* (to grieve or cause distress).\n\nThe main modern sense of \"laziness, love of ease\" dates to around **1710**, perhaps reflecting the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcdd Synonyms\n\nSynonyms for indolent include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcac Example Sentences\n\n- \"She abhorred games of all kinds, suggesting that they appeal to the **indolent** without the imagination to invent their own learning and growing activities.\"\n- \"Sometimes clashes arise between a caring wife and an **indolent** husband or son.\"\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Pronunciation\n**IN-duh-lunt** /\u02c8\u026and\u0259l\u0259nt/\n\nIn summary, **indolent** primarily describes someone who is habitually lazy and avoids effort, and in a medical context, it describes a condition that is slow-moving or causes little pain.",
